An apple orchard smells like wine;
A succory flower is blue;
Until Grief touched these eyes of mine,
Such things I never knew.

Even a king's house tumbles down;
   A roof is soon forgot;
So was it once in Corinth town,
   So once in Camelot.

**C**rown the day with songs of glory,  
**E**very heart repeats the lay;  
**N**oble sires, we tell your story,  
**T**rumpets greet the hundredth day.  

**E**mblemed on thy broad blue banner,  
**N**erved by memories bright with flame;  
**N**ow we shield it, in like manner,  
**I**n God's sight, thy sacred name.  

**A**ll our hopes and prayers attend thee;  
**L**ong live thou, our land, still free!

To Thee, O God, the sunset banners flow;  
And all the clouds grow tender in Thy grace;  
They fold the day upon the sea's still face,  
Till night receives Thy last, departing glow.

A mother’s love endures through every change;
It watches still when other hearts grow cold;
Through years of care and sorrow it remains,
And follows where the child may choose to go.

O'er thine abysses Thought may brood serene;
Or soar triumphant over Space and Time,
Moulding thy plastic elements at will.
